  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="15d9cce3-626b-5aa3-82ed-b16dc1ffdc24" activityNameId="03f270f1-9344-55af-982f-2df983c08ed0" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Electricity, at 1005MW wind farm, offshore, 15MW turbines</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Electricity, at 1005MW wind farm, offshore, 15MW turbines
UUID=350a9796-22ba-37ac-8733-4dba1db18e3b
Electricity from wind farm, fed to grid. Includes the operation of the wind farm with the necessary change of gear oil. A leackage of 5% of SF6 is assumed over the entire life cycle of the wind farm.
The modelled 1005 MW wind farm is a fictitious offshore wind farm based on the Renewable Energy Material Property Database, REMPD (Cooperman et al., 2023), consisting of 67 IEA reference offshore wind turbines with a nominal capacity of 15 MW and monopile substructures, as well as 1 offshore substation and 1 land-based substation..
The distance to service port is assumed to be 50 km. Total length of export cables is 210 km (assuming 3 parallel export cables of 70 km each). The total length of array cables is 133 km.
The model uses a capacity factor of 34.1%, representing the average capacity factor of offshore wind farms in Europe between 2016-2024 (source: ENTSO-E).
Assumed lifetime is 25 years. Annual electricity production is 3004 GWh, applying a capacity factor of 34.1%.
Location: 
Technology: 
Time period: 
Production volume: 
Sampling: 
Extrapolations: 
Uncertainty adjustments: none
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_350a9796-22ba-37ac-8733-4dba1db18e3b.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
